From 3e8ff5b27c8195b768d2e775123263afa49478de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andy <andylee77@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:36:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08/13] infinity6c: report ae_luma on the 0..255 scale

The upstream exposure contract, and ric's night_luma, are 0..255. CUS3A's
preAvgY is 10-bit: on the MC-A85 bench AE settled every IR-lit scene at
410, where Ingenic's GetAeLuma settles ~100, and a dim room at ceiling
gain read 110-130. Passed through as-is, every luma comparison in ric
ran four times too bright. Shift it down to 8 bits.
---
 src/infinity6c/hal_isp.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/infinity6c/hal_isp.c b/src/infinity6c/hal_isp.c
index 0a86af2..5351568 100644
--- a/src/infinity6c/hal_isp.c
+++ b/src/infinity6c/hal_isp.c
@@ -186,8 +186,11 @@ int hal_isp_get_exposure(void *ctx, rss_exposure_t *exposure)
     exposure->total_gain = (uint32_t)(((uint64_t)ae.sensorGain * ae.ispGain) >> 10);
     exposure->valid_mask |= RSS_EXPOSURE_VALID_TOTAL_GAIN;
     /* preAvgY comes out of the same status read, so it is as valid as the
-     * gain is -- including when the frame really is black. */
-    exposure->ae_luma = ae.preAvgY;
+     * gain is -- including when the frame really is black. It is 10-bit
+     * (AE settles a lit scene at ~410, where Ingenic's 0..255 GetAeLuma
+     * settles ~100; MC-A85 bench 2026-10-24), and ae_luma is 0..255, the
+     * scale ric's night_luma is set in. */
+    exposure->ae_luma = ae.preAvgY >> 2;
     exposure->valid_mask |= RSS_EXPOSURE_VALID_AE_LUMA;
 
     have_awb =
